program order_blotter

-- ============================================================
-- Order blotter: a claim recorded in error, on a record that
-- never forgets
-- ============================================================
--
-- A trading desk keeps a blotter: the list of orders it placed, the
-- fills it got back, the cancels it sent. Each line is an event that
-- happened, so the blotter is append only - nothing on it is ever
-- removed. That is the right property for an audit record, and it
-- raises one hard question: what does the desk do when a line was
-- written by mistake?
--
-- This example is built around that question. A cancel was recorded
-- on an order that had in truth been filled. The cancel is true as an
-- event, the desk did send it, and wrong as a conclusion, the order
-- was not open to cancel. Retracting it is not allowed: the blotter
-- is append only, and the runtime refuses any act that tries. The
-- correction pattern the gallery already teaches, where a figure is
-- superseded and a pointer moves to the new one, does not fit either:
-- a cancel is not a figure, and there is no pointer.
--
-- The answer is an erratum: a claim about a claim. It names the
-- recorded line by its own identity, gives a reason, and is itself
-- append only. From then on the record says two things at once, both
-- permanently true: this cancel was recorded and admitted, and this
-- cancel is not to be treated as standing. Every rule that cares
-- whether an order is cancelled reads the second; a reader who asks
-- what the desk recorded reads the first. What was actually true, the
-- fill, then goes on the record through the ordinary act, once the
-- erroneous cancel no longer stands in its way.
--
-- An erratum records an authorised correction. It does not prove what
-- happened outside; it says who decided the line was wrong and why.

-- An order placed on a book, for a quantity.
predicate Order(order: Subject, book: Subject, qty: Decimal)
    unique by (order)
    append only

-- A fill the venue reported against an order. Each fill has its own
-- identity, so two fills on one order are two lines.
predicate Fill(order: Subject, fill_id: Subject, qty: Decimal)
    unique by (fill_id)
    append only

-- A cancel the desk sent. Its own identity matters here more than
-- anywhere: an erratum will name this line, and only this line, by it.
predicate Cancel(order: Subject, cancel_id: Subject)
    unique by (cancel_id)
    append only

-- The erratum. It names a recorded line by the line's identity, never
-- by the order the line describes, and gives a reason. One erratum per
-- line, recorded once and never withdrawn: if the erratum itself turns
-- out to be wrong, what was true is recorded again as a fresh line.
predicate RecordedInError(claim_id: Subject, reason: Subject)
    unique by (claim_id)
    append only

-- Who may record an erratum on a book's lines.
predicate MayCorrect(book: Subject, corrector: Subject)
    unique by (book)

-- A cancel that stands: recorded, and not marked in error. Rules read
-- cancels through this and nothing else, so an erratum changes what
-- every rule sees without touching the line it marks.
define standing_cancel(order, cancel_id):
    Cancel(order, cancel_id) and not RecordedInError(cancel_id, _)

-- A fill or a cancel belongs to an order that was placed.
invariant fill_has_an_order:
    Fill(order, fill_id, _) implies Order(order, _, _)

invariant cancel_has_an_order:
    Cancel(order, cancel_id) implies Order(order, _, _)

-- An erratum names a cancel that exists. Without this, a mark could
-- be recorded against nothing.
invariant erratum_names_a_cancel:
    RecordedInError(cancel_id, _) implies Cancel(_, cancel_id)

-- A cancel closes only an unfilled order: a fill and a standing cancel
-- never meet. The rule starts from the recorded cancel, so the runtime
-- knows which order and which cancel a change concerns and checks
-- that case alone; a rule written only over standing cancels would be
-- checked over the whole blotter on every change.
invariant a_cancel_closes_only_an_unfilled_order:
    Cancel(order, cancel_id) and Fill(order, _, _) implies not standing_cancel(order, cancel_id)

-- At most one standing cancel per order. A declared uniqueness cannot
-- say this, because it sees every recorded cancel and knows nothing of
-- errata; so the rule is written out, over standing cancels.
invariant one_standing_cancel_per_order:
    Cancel(order, a) and Cancel(order, b) and standing_cancel(order, a) and standing_cancel(order, b) implies a = b

-- Fills never exceed the order.
invariant fills_within_the_order:
    Order(order, _, qty) implies sum(q | Fill(order, _, q)) <= qty

-- The acts.

transformation place(order, book, qty):
    require not Order(order: order, ..)
    admit Order(order, book, qty)

-- A fill carries no gate of its own: the rules above refuse a fill on
-- an unknown order, past the order's quantity, or against a standing
-- cancel, each by name.
transformation fill(order, fill_id, qty):
    admit Fill(order, fill_id, qty)

transformation cancel(order, cancel_id):
    admit Cancel(order, cancel_id)

-- The first appointment names a book's corrector; from then on the
-- appointment cannot be replaced. Who makes that first appointment is
-- the desk's own trust root, outside this record.
transformation appoint_corrector(book, corrector):
    require not MayCorrect(book, _)
    admit MayCorrect(book, corrector)

-- Recording an erratum. The caller names the cancel, nothing else: the
-- order and the book come from the record, so authority is checked
-- against the book the cancel really belongs to, not a book the caller
-- picks.
transformation record_erratum(cancel_id, reason):
    bind Cancel(order, cancel_id)
    bind Order(order, book, _)
    require MayCorrect(book, actor)
    admit RecordedInError(cancel_id, reason)

-- What this example deliberately does not cover: a fill recorded in
-- error (the same shape, an erratum naming the fill); partial fills
-- and cancelling the unfilled remainder; and how a desk's trust root
-- appoints the first corrector.
